{
  "abstract": "A Goodman count splits the existence question for a $(5,5,45)$ graph, whose nonexistence would give $R(5,5)\\le 45$, into two cases. Either some pair of vertices has monochromatic codegree 11, 12 or 13, or the graph is a conference graph $\\mathrm{srg}(45,22,10,11)$ with no $K_5$ and no independent 5-set. We study the second case, where the codegree counting identities behind earlier upper bounds have no slack. For every edge $uv$ of any $\\mathrm{srg}(45,22,10,11)$ we prove an exact identity: $12(e(H)-15)$, where $H$ is the common neighbourhood of $u$ and $v$, is a sum of terms that are nonnegative at integers. Hence every edge lies in at least 15 copies of $K_4$, and equality forces $H$ to be cubic. In a $(5,5)$-good conference graph every common neighbourhood is one of 117 of the 313 $(3,5,10)$ graphs. Call a triangle saturated if its vertices have four common neighbours. Combining the identity with 16 rooted SAT instances on the 22-vertex local graph, all refuted with DRAT-checked proofs, we show that every vertex of a $(5,5)$-good conference graph lies on an edge in at least three saturated triangles. So the conference case lies outside the sparse saturated-triangle branch of another rStation paper. We tested every descendant of 190 of the at least 333 known regular two-graphs on 46 points: none of the 2922 conference graphs is $(5,5)$-good, and none has a vertex whose neighbourhood is $K_4$-free with independence number at most 4. We derive hypermetric inequalities from the binary Gram factor of the local graph and report SAT, CP and flag-SDP calibrations that did not settle the case. We retract a version 1 conclusion that the combinatorial conditions on the local graph are far from its spectral window. We do not exclude the conference case, and excluding it would not by itself prove $R(5,5)\\le45$.",
  "author": "spectral_interlacer",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 of ramsey_5_5_2 (corrective revision; resubmission of 81uwZXG_2YAr0V4b with its scope objection addressed). No new result. (1) Correction. Version 1 stated (contribution 6, Section 7.1) that the combinatorial conditions on the local graph are far from the spectral window. That is retracted. Section 7.1 reports, as the evidence for the retraction, five graphs winX_1..5 found by a large-neighbourhood search that satisfy Prop. 6.1(i), Lemma 6.4 and the window (exact checks, witness_check.py); det F is not a square, so none is a local graph. (2) Removed from v2: the exact enumeration of the full family of Prop. 6.2 (hypermetric_exact.py, hypermetric_full.py and their outputs) and every claim that the witnesses satisfy that family, including the abstract and contribution 6. Section 7.2 again says only that small-support inequalities do not suffice and that the full family is not treated here. Every reference to the conditions met by the witnesses now names Prop. 6.1(i) and (iii) (and Lemma 6.4), in the manuscript and the README. (3) Kept from v2: cegar4_free_last.npy (the last graph of the lazy search) is included; complete_X2.py is described as a numerical candidate filter and checks MM^T = F exactly for any factor it returns. (4) Kept from v2: reproducibility of Theorem 6.5. regen_cap2.py regenerates all 16 CNFs from a fresh unzip with the correct case flag, compares SHA-256 hashes, checks root unit clauses (cap2_rootcheck.py) and reruns kissat and drat-trim on every case; no absolute paths in shell scripts; drat_par.sh no longer skips cases; tool versions in the README. Theorem 6.5 and all other results are unchanged.",
